`satisfies` and `max_satisfying` are now implemented in `semver_range.py`, and my checks against the spec pass.

I turned the spec's examples into asserts and ran them. They cover:
- every range form: caret, tilde, X-ranges, hyphen ranges, comparators with spaces, and `||`;
- the empty range;
- the prerelease rule;
- the ordering chain of prerelease versions;
- build metadata being ignored;
- `max_satisfying` returning the first of two equal-precedence strings.

I also ran negative cases for each rule, and they all raise `ValueError`. These included `~>1.2`, `>=1.2.3 -`, `1.2.3.4`, `^`, `>=a.b.c`, `1.x.3`, leading zeros, bare `1 - 2 - 3`, and invalid version strings.

My first run of the checks failed twice, and both were mistakes in my checks. One expected value was wrong (`2.4.0` does satisfy `1.2.3 - 2`), and I tested prerelease ordering through `satisfies`, which the prerelease rule gets in the way of. I fixed the checks, not the code.

The spec doesn't say whether `>=v1.2.3` is valid. I accept one leading `v` after an operator but reject `=v1.2.3` and `==1`.

The checks are in `/tmp/t.py`, outside the project directory. There were no existing tests to run.

